w to share my feelings if i've  never modeled i've never seen it modeled  i didn't see my parents married i never  saw  arguments i never saw a conversation and  communicate i didn't know how that stuff  worked so there's a part of they'll say  if i'm never taught how to do something  or if i don't develop a life skill how  do i then go ahead and develop that and  i pushed people away you know there was  a lot where i wanted to live this jekyll  and hyde life  i had this ego i had this competitive  spirit i wanted to do everything bigger  than everyone else i mean that's part of  my personality trait in the midst of my  gambling i had to have if my friends had  bet a hundred dollars i had to bet 500.  if they ordered one dinner i had to  order two i mean i always had to one-up  somebody else  and you look at some of the personality  traits for people who struggle with  addiction specific to gambling and it's  that competitive drive it's that fear of  failure it's that that risk-taking  mentality all of which has just been a  part of my life it's part of my dna and  that's why addiction is so complex it's  biology it's genetics it's who you grew  up around who you make friends with  but i do take ownership i take ownership  because i need to do that for myself so  that i can forgive myself as well in  that i'm not a i'm a flawed human being  you know i'm going to make mistakes  every day  i hurt people along the way  i asked forgiveness from a lot of people  some of which  i didn't get but that's out of my  control and i think about that serenity  prayer all the time of kind of  separating you know knowing what we can  control and knowing what's out of our  control you know having that wisdom to  know the difference between the two  but i had to take ownership i mean it  starts with taking ownership and  accepting accountability and  responsibility and then finding a sense  of usefulness and purpose in my life  which was lacking for so long i had so  much shame and so much guilt until i was  able to start talking about it it  started freeing me from that that mental  prison that i was struggling in  yeah gambling is a  an interesting  addiction i i went to a couple gambling  anonymous gambling anonymous meetings in  vegas when i was there once looking for  gamblers to interview and is very  difficult because they don't want to  talk typically but yeah one of the  things i heard in the meetings over and  over again was  from the gamblers is i have an off  switch but i don't have enough i have an  on switch but i don't have an off switch  yeah  you know it's funny i often hear people  talk about that i've even heard renowned  doctors talk about that once the  addiction switches on it stays on and  and that's that's across the board for  all addictions you know what's  interesting with gambling is what i  mentioned before is the fact that it's  hard to  stop an activity where you're getting  rewarded every so often and if you look  the brain of a gambling a person with a  gambling problem lights up in a similar  regions as someone who has cocaine  addiction the similar areas are  activated and so all of a sudden i'm not  putting anything in my body yet i'm  getting such a buzz and such a high off  this this behavioral process so it  impacts the brain and it's not just the  winning because gambling a lot of people  think gambling is a money problem  gambling's an emotional problem gambling  people with gambling problems are either  using it for action or escape you know i  started as an action gambler i love the  rush but after september 11th i became  an escaped gambler because i didn't know  how to deal with lice is it the  adrenaline of the risk  yeah there's a couple of things it's the  dopamine boost that you keep getting and  that adrenaline it's the anticipation as  well as the participation so it's  thinking about what the next bet will be  thinking about what the outcome will be  and also then actively watching it take  place  so if you're  wagering on the internet or betting on  sports nowadays you can bet on sports  you can put a new bet every 20 seconds  you can bet on the next uh serve in a  tennis match the next pitch in a  baseball game so to to like get that  granular with it i'm anticipating what  it will be i'm hoping for a good outcome  if it happens great it registers and i  move on to the next one but if it  doesn't  i'm still buzzing i'm still feeling  really high and i'm just going to move  on to the next one anyway so either way  i'm getting an adrenaline rush and a  dopamine hit because you work in  prevention or  recovery of gambling i mean what what  kind of numbers do you know about how  common it is so the the  here's a couple of issues in the united  states with gambling is that there's no  federal funding to support treatment  or prevention we have  funding for drug and alcohol prevention  and treatment and services there's no  federal funding for gambling it's all  done at the state level so the research  is limited but the last research the  latest research estimates that roughly  two percent of americans have a  full-blown gambling disorder  now from a percentage perspective that's  not a lot but if you think about how  many people in the united states three  billion people in the united states  two percent with that six hundred  thousand people or something like that  i think the numbers are growing oh  actually no would be higher than that be  like six million six million people six  million six million so the numbers are  growing though and here's where we're  starting to see concern since 2018  sports betting is now starting to become  legalized state by state passport was  repealed that allowed each state to  decide how and if they want to enforce  regulate and and  govern sports betting within its own  state borders new jersey was one of the  first ones to market with that what we  saw in new jersey  people calling 800 gambler helpline the  number of people with sports betting  related problems ballooned from four  percent annually up to about 16 percent  annually four-fold increase so we're  starting to see more people starting to  exhibit gambling related harms we're  also pushing it out to the younger  demographic our college students  you know people in high school who  shouldn't even be gambling yet are  partnering with their older siblings or  parents and placing bets on sporting  contests works we're normalizing the  expansion of gambling amongst our youth  i anticipate we're going to see those  numbers grow as time goes on that's why  we have to get into the schools at an  early age prevention is all about  starting soon you know schools allocate  funding for prevention for drug and  alcohol but when you talk about gambling  it's just not a problem that they see  because you can't see a gambling problem  that well yet we know people walk around  struggling with gambling addiction and  it's the loved ones who also suffer  because gambling has some of the largest  financial consequences of the of the  major addictions as well as some of the  highest suicidal ideation and attempt  yeah it's a it's an interesting  addiction  i'm thankful i never um  i think about the suicide piece  that's something that never crossed my  mind and i've lost friends since  september 11th when i lost a lot of  friends i lost uh two more to suicide  after september 11th they couldn't deal  with life and then along my journey of  recovery in the last 12 years  uh i've lost friends of suicide from  gambling addiction because they just  that they can't they just can't take it  anymore and i remember one in particular  was on  i was at a meeting in north jersey some  number of years ago on july 3rd the  night before independence day  and i remember this young man was a wall  street executive like myself uh in  recovery was struggling was still living  at home couldn't move out because he had  no money left  and he grew up in a family where they  gambled every friday night so he's at a  ga meeting trying to get well  and he would go home to an environment  where they were playing poker and it was  a family where it was the father the  uncle the siblings and every time he'd  come in they'd say come on sit down  you're playing and they'd force him at  the table  he couldn't take it anymore he ended up  taking his own life on july 4th  and i remember him talking about giving  his therapy that night talking about  freedom from addiction and independence  and he says i think there's only one way  i can get free of this thing and i just  remember that  none of us knew at the time that he was  talking about  completing  suicide  but  looking backwards  that's probably what he was referring to  that was hard  dan what would you say is the most  important thing you've learned from all  of this  i'm thankful  i think um  coming out of september 11th having  opportunities in my life from baseball  to working in the city to  meeting just amazing people every day  and hearing what people can accomplish  with what little they have at times  i think i'm just thankful  i think we need to really work on  listening more than we speak that's  something i  i'm here talking with you today but it's  something i really try to do  because people just want a voice they  want to be heard and they they want to  have an outlet and that's something that  i realized that i don't think i had  so i try to give that i try to i try to  give more compassion versus comparison  that's a big thing that i focus on today  when we talk about social media and the  age of technology we see so many people  falling for that comparison model you  know i'm not good enough i'm not smart  enough i'm not pretty enough i'm not as  good as blank right comparing yourself  to someone else  letting people know that that you are  worthy  you you do deserve to be here you are  right where you're supposed to be in  this moment and just trying to work with  people to help give them hope and  encouragement  you know one of the things i realized i  was very thankful that i was reconnected  with my higher power spending time back  in the church something my grandparents  instilled in me at an early age but i  always revolted against it  was confusing to me i didn't want to be  a part of religion i didn't want  anything to do with religion but what i  realized over time and one of my biggest  takeaways is that it's not religion it's  a relationship  and in recovery from addiction we need  to form  relationships  because when we're in active addiction  we want isolation relationship is the  furthest thing from our mind when we're  an act of addiction because of the shame  and stigma  so today as i go around the country and  as i work in the field that i do and i'm  very blessed and thankful for that  i've helped people focus on relationship  help people realize that they are good  enough and they're worthy enough and  that it's okay to not be okay and just  always have a trusted friend by their  side  excellent dan what a great talk thank  you so much you're welcome thank you i'm  glad you're doing better thank you thank  you  you
 

